# What never belongs in the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites: a smaller context uploads
# faster, AND a `node_modules` copied in from the host would shadow the one
# `npm ci` just installed — with whatever platform-specific binaries your
# laptop or Termux happened to build. `npm ci` inside the image is the only
# install that should ever matter.

# ---- Dependencies. Installed in the image, never copied into it.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# ---- Build output. Produced by the image's own build stages. A stale
# admin/dist copied in from the host is the failure mode worth naming: the
# image would serve a backoffice older than the code it was built from, with
# nothing in the logs saying so.
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# ---- Secrets. A .env in the context ends up in an image layer, and layers
# are readable by anyone who can pull the image. Configuration comes from
# Northflank's environment at run time.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# ---- Expo / native. The mobile app is not part of this image at all. The
# generated native projects are large, and a keystore in a layer is a signing
# key handed to anyone with the image.
.expo/
**/.expo/
android/
**/android/
ios/
**/ios/
*.apk
*.aab
*.jks
*.keystore
apk-parts/

# ---- The mobile app itself. Nothing in the server image reads it.
mobile/

# ---- Git. `.git` is often the largest single thing in the context, and its
# history can contain secrets that were committed and later removed.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# ---- Documentation and planning. No effect on the build.
*.md
docs/

# ---- Logs, editor and OS noise.
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---- Render's own build entrypoint. Superseded by this Dockerfile; kept in
# the repository until the Northflank deploy is verified. See NORTHFLANK.md.
render.yaml
render-build.sh

# ---- Docker's own files.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
